.TH "CAPNG_LAST_CAP" "3" "November 2026" "Red Hat" "Libcap-ng API"
.SH NAME
capng_last_cap \- report the highest capability number of the running kernel
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B #include <cap-ng.h>
.sp
unsigned int capng_last_cap(void);

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

capng_last_cap returns the number of the highest capability supported by the
running kernel. The library determines this value once when it is loaded, from
.I /proc/sys/kernel/cap_last_cap
when it is available and otherwise by probing the kernel.

Use this value, not the
.B CAP_LAST_CAP
macro from the build headers, as the upper bound when iterating over
capabilities. A kernel newer than the headers an application was built
against may support capabilities the headers do not name.
.BR capng_capability_to_name (3)
returns a name of the form
.B cap_\fIN\fP
for such a capability, and every capability up to the returned value can be
queried and updated.

.SH "RETURN VALUE"

This returns the highest valid capability number. It cannot fail.

.SH "SEE ALSO"

.BR capng_capability_to_name (3),
.BR capng_have_capability (3),
.BR capabilities (7)

.SH AUTHOR
Steve Grubb
